# 20. Explain to an Interviewer — 60-Second Script 🎤

> K-Nearest Neighbors is a supervised, non-parametric, instance-based machine learning algorithm. Instead of learning explicit model parameters during training, it stores the training examples and makes predictions based on the nearest neighbors of a new data point. For classification, it usually uses majority voting, while for regression it can average the values of neighboring points. The definition of similarity depends on a distance metric such as Euclidean or Manhattan distance. Choosing K is important because a small K can overfit to noise, while a large K can underfit. KNN is also highly sensitive to feature scaling and can suffer from the curse of dimensionality, making it less suitable for very high-dimensional or extremely large datasets.

---

# 21. Top 3 Interview Questions 🎯

## Q1. Why is KNN called a lazy learner?

**Answer:**

> KNN is called a lazy learner because it performs little or no explicit model training. It mainly stores the training data and postpones most computation until prediction time.

---

## Q2. Why is feature scaling important in KNN?

**Answer:**

> KNN relies on distance calculations. If one feature has a much larger numerical range than another, it can dominate the distance calculation and unfairly influence the prediction.

---

## Q3. What happens when K is too small or too large?

**Answer:**

> A small K makes the model sensitive to local noise and can cause overfitting, while a large K creates smoother decision boundaries but may underfit by ignoring important local patterns.

